body:
Although this design can achieve the correct completions functionality, currently, for different shells, a separate set of functions is implemented to handle it. We can see that these functions all contain a large number of strings, and most of the logic is the same. When new functions need to be added, a lot of modifications to the strings are required, which is unacceptable for maintainability. In the future, there might even be different completion methods for different subcommands.

Specifically, this function is to provide the shell with the relevant content in specific subcommands. For example, vex list needs to provide all saved configurations. However, in the current design, this function signature only receives a bin_name, and then all processing is carried out by the subsequent large string. This makes the code's readability very poor.
```rust
fn print_bash_dynamic_completion(bin_name: &str) 
```

A feasible design is to implement for each shell:
```rust
trait DynamicCompletion {
    fn list_command(&self, /* ... */);
    fn exec_command(&self, /* ... */);
    fn run_command(&self, /* ... */);
    // ...
}
```

